 ```
 
+#### `place-within-workspace`
+
+<sup>Since: next release</sup>
+
+Show a *background* or *bottom* layer surface on one workspace only, instead of on every workspace.
+
+Background and bottom layer surfaces normally render once behind every workspace of their output: they move and zoom with the workspaces, but each workspace shows the same surface.
+A surface with `place-within-workspace` renders behind its own workspace alone: in the normal view, during workspace switch animations (where it slides with its workspace), and in the [Overview](./Overview.md).
+It also gets input only while its workspace is on screen.
+On any other workspace it isn't drawn at all.
+
+The value is a workspace reference, read like the one `niri msg action focus-workspace` takes: a number is a 1-based index on the surface's output, anything else is a workspace name.
+`$1`, `${1}` or `${name}` expand to the captures of the `namespace` regex that matched, so one rule can bind a whole family of surfaces.
+
+A surface bound to a workspace never goes into the overview backdrop (`place-within-backdrop` is ignored for it).
+Surfaces without this property keep the usual behavior.
+
+```kdl
+// Per-workspace wallpapers: a client creates one background surface per workspace
+// it wants to decorate, named wallpaper@<workspace>.
+layer-rule {
+    match namespace="^wallpaper@(.+)$"
+
+    place-within-workspace "$1"
+}
+```
+
+Since only workspaces that are on screen draw their surfaces, a client can keep just those mapped (the active workspace and its neighbors, or every visible one while the overview is open) and destroy the rest.
+
 #### `baba-is-float`
 
 <sup>Since: 25.05</sup>
